he observations section and recommend it as a separate action in Stage 3, even if it's not formally a Stage 1 candidate. Now I need to decide whether to keep ترشيح or remove it, since the critical issue is whether ترشيح actually fits the filtering sense.\n\nLooking at the definitions, ترشيح maps to filtration—the physical separation of solid particles from fluid using a filter medium—which is fundamentally different from leaching, which involves dissolution and transport of soluble substances by water. The Arabic dictionaries consistently support this distinction, so ترشيح shouldn't be in this synset. There's a related entry mapping it to percolation in water engineering, which is closer to leaching but still not the same thing. I'm removing ترشيح from the synset and now considering whether الارتشاح could work instead.\n\nThe entry for ارتشاح shows it paired with غسل (شطف) for leaching, but it's a compound entry rather than standing alone. After reviewing all the candidates, I'm finalizing my decisions: keeping غَسْل التُّرْبَة as confirmed, and removing ترشيح, النَّضْح, الإِرْشَاح, التَّرَشُّح, and الاسْتِنْضَاح since none of them have solid standalone evidence for leaching.\n\nI should also note that نَضّ appears in the Civil Engineering dictionary as an explicit synonym for leaching, so that's worth considering. Now I'm working on the definition for غَسْل التُّرْبَة. The current definition relies on ترشيح (which actually means filtration) and doesn't capture the soil-specific or natural aspects of leaching, so I'm drafting a more precise terminological definition that emphasizes water percolating through soil or solid material to dissolve and remove soluble substances. I'm tracing the etymological roots through classical Arabic sources—the root غسل (washing/purifying) comes from Maqayis and fundamentally means cleansing and purification, while ترب (earth/soil) derives from the same lexicographic tradition, establishing how the compound term غسل التربة literally c